-		<English>[TAB]The Changping Granary (Changpingcang), often called an "ever-normal granary", was not just a warehouse: it was a public tool for managing the grain market. Already in the 1st century BC, officials in the Han realm treated grain prices as a political danger in both directions: when prices fell too low after harvest, farmers suffered; when prices rose during scarcity, city households and the poor were squeezed. The solution was simple in principle and demanding in practice. Counties were ordered to maintain reserve granaries, buying grain when it was cheap to prevent a collapse in prices, then releasing grain when it was dear to pull prices back down and keep food within reach.[PARAGRAPH:1]Over later dynasties the idea persisted and expanded, with large-scale county systems especially associated with the Qing. The goal was steady supply: grain on hand for local shortfalls, fewer price shocks, and fewer panics when harvests failed. By moving grain through official buying and selling, the state aimed to keep the market "ever normal" - stable enough that ordinary people could plan their lives and survive bad years without chaos.[PARAGRAPH:1]In a rice-growing city, such a system meant more than full storehouses. It meant cleaner reserves, fewer losses to spoilage, and a calmer table when the staple harvest was good.</English>
+		<English>[TAB]The Changping Granary (Changpingcang), also known as an "ever-normal granary," served as a government mechanism for grain price stabilization in imperial China. Dating to the Han Dynasty, the system addressed the dual problem of agricultural price volatility: low post-harvest prices harmed rural producers, while scarcity-driven highs burdened urban consumers and the poor. The operational model required county-level administrations to maintain strategic grain reserves, purchasing surplus during periods of low prices to support farmer incomes, then distributing stock during shortages to moderate market rates and maintain food accessibility.[PARAGRAPH:1]The institution persisted across successive dynasties, with extensive county networks particularly developed during the Qing Dynasty. Its primary functions included securing local grain supplies, mitigating price shocks, and preventing social instability during crop failures. Through state intervention in grain markets?buying when prices declined and selling when they rose?the system sought to maintain price equilibrium sufficient for household economic planning and subsistence security during adverse agricultural conditions.[PARAGRAPH:1]In rice-cultivating regions, the Changping system contributed to reduced grain spoilage, improved storage practices, and enhanced food security during favorable harvest cycles.</English>
